= The Typed Imp Language Specification
:toc: left
:toclevels: 2
:sectnums:
:icons: font

Typed Imp is a small typed imperative language. Its syntax covers
integer and boolean literals, variables, arithmetic and boolean
operators, sequencing, conditionals, `while` loops, and typed variable
declarations. The static semantics assigns types to expressions and
commands under a typing context; the dynamic semantics evaluates
expressions and executes commands under an environment that maps
variables to values.

== Syntax

The abstract syntax of Typed Imp distinguishes types, literals,
variables, expressions, commands, and whole programs.

[[type]]
[[literal]]
[[id]]
[[expr]]
[[command]]
[[prog]]
[source,bison]
----
syntax type = 
   | INT
   | BOOL
   | type -> type

syntax literal = 
   | _NUM int
   | _BOOL bool

syntax id = 
   | _ID text

syntax expr = 
   | literal
   | id
   | ! expr
   | expr + expr
   | expr <= expr
   | expr && expr
   | FUN (type id) -> type {expr}
   | expr (expr)

syntax command = 
   | SKIP
   | type id = expr
   | id = expr
   | IF expr THEN command ELSE command END
   | WHILE expr DO command END
   | command ; command

syntax prog = command
----

== Auxiliary definitions

These auxiliary functions are shared by the static and dynamic
semantics.

=== Lookup

Lookup over an association list takes a list of `(key, value)` pairs and
returns the first value bound to the requested key.

[[lookup]]
[.sidebar-title]
****
xref:lookup[$lookup]:

* Lookup of `K~query~` in `pair<K, V>^{asterisk}^`
****

****
. If `pair<K, V>^{asterisk}^` is an empty list:
 .. Return `·`.
. Otherwise, if `pair<K, V>^{asterisk}^` is `K~h~ → V~h~ {two-colons} ( K~t~ → V~t~ )^{asterisk}^`:
 .. `K~h~` must equal `K~query~`.
 .. Return `V~h~`.
. Otherwise:
 .. `pair<K, V>^{asterisk}^` must be a non-empty list.
 .. Let `K~h~ → V~h~ {two-colons} ( K~t~ → V~t~ )^{asterisk}^` be `pair<K, V>^{asterisk}^`.
 .. Return xref:lookup[lookup of `K~query~` in `( K~t~ → V~t~ )^{asterisk}^`].
****

== Static semantics

The typing judgments characterise well-formed expressions and commands
under a typing context that maps each in-scope variable to its declared
type.

=== Expressions

[[Check_expr]]
[.sidebar-title]
****
xref:Check_expr[Check_expr]:

* Typechecking `expr` under context `tenv`.
****

****
. If `expr` is `literal`:
 .. If `literal` is the integer `i`:
  ... Result in `INT`.
 .. Otherwise, if `literal` is the boolean `b`:
  ... Result in `BOOL`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `x`:
 .. Let `t` be xref:lookup[lookup of `x` in `tenv`], which must exist.
 .. Result in `t`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `! e`:
 .. Let `type` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type` must be `BOOL`.
 .. Result in `BOOL`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `e~l~ {plus} e~r~`:
 .. Let `type` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e~l~` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type` must be `INT`.
 .. Let `type'` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e~r~` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type'` must be `INT`.
 .. Result in `INT`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `e~l~ {lt}= e~r~`:
 .. Let `type` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e~l~` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type` must be `INT`.
 .. Let `type'` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e~r~` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type'` must be `INT`.
 .. Result in `BOOL`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `e~l~ {amp}{amp} e~r~`:
 .. Let `type` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e~l~` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type` must be `BOOL`.
 .. Let `type'` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e~r~` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type'` must be `BOOL`.
 .. Result in `BOOL`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `FUN (t~arg~ x~arg~) -> t~ret~ +++{+++e+++}+++`:
 .. Let `type` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e` under context `x~arg~ → t~arg~ {two-colons} tenv`].
 .. `type` must equal `t~ret~`.
 .. Result in `t~arg~ -> t~ret~`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `e~f~ (e~a~)`:
 .. Let `type` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e~f~` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type` must be `t~arg~ -> t~ret~`.
 .. Let `type'` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e~a~` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type'` must equal `t~arg~`.
 .. Result in `t~ret~`.
****

=== Commands

[[Check_command]]
[.sidebar-title]
****
xref:Check_command[Check_command]:

* Typechecking `command` under context `tenv`.
****

****
. If `command` is `SKIP`:
 .. Result in `tenv`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `t x = e`:
 .. Let `type` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type` must equal `t`.
 .. Result in `x → t {two-colons} tenv`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `x = e`:
 .. Let `t` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e` under context `tenv`].
 .. xref:lookup[lookup of `x` in `tenv`] must equal `t`.
 .. Result in `tenv`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `IF e THEN c~1~ ELSE c~2~ END`:
 .. Let `type` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type` must be `BOOL`.
 .. Let `tenv~1~` be the result of xref:Check_command[typechecking `c~1~` under context `tenv`].
 .. Let `tenv~2~` be the result of xref:Check_command[typechecking `c~2~` under context `tenv`].
 .. Result in `tenv`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `WHILE e DO c END`:
 .. Let `type` be the result of xref:Check_expr[typechecking `e` under context `tenv`].
 .. `type` must be `BOOL`.
 .. Let `tenv~1~` be the result of xref:Check_command[typechecking `c` under context `tenv`].
 .. Result in `tenv`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `c~1~ ; c~2~`:
 .. Let `tenv~1~` be the result of xref:Check_command[typechecking `c~1~` under context `tenv`].
 .. Let `tenv~2~` be the result of xref:Check_command[typechecking `c~2~` under context `tenv~1~`].
 .. Result in `tenv~2~`.
****

== Dynamic semantics

The evaluation judgments give a big-step semantics. Expressions reduce
to values; commands transform an environment to an environment.

=== Expressions

[[Eval_expr]]
[.sidebar-title]
****
xref:Eval_expr[Eval_expr]:

* Evaluating `expr` in environment `env`.
****

****
. If `expr` is `literal`:
 .. If `literal` is the integer `i`:
  ... Result in the integer `i`.
 .. Otherwise, if `literal` is the boolean `b`:
  ... Result in the boolean `b`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `x`:
 .. Let `v` be xref:lookup[lookup of `x` in `env`], which must exist.
 .. Result in `v`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `! e`:
 .. Let `value` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e` in environment `env`].
 .. Let `literal` be `value`, which must be a `literal`.
 .. `literal` must be the boolean `b~e~`.
 .. Let `b` be not `b~e~`.
 .. Result in the boolean `b`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `e~l~ {plus} e~r~`:
 .. Let `value` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e~l~` in environment `env`].
 .. Let `literal` be `value`, which must be a `literal`.
 .. `literal` must be the integer `i~l~`.
 .. Let `value'` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e~r~` in environment `env`].
 .. Let `literal'` be `value'`, which must be a `literal`.
 .. `literal'` must be the integer `i~r~`.
 .. Let `i` be `i~l~` + `i~r~`.
 .. Result in the integer `i`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `e~l~ {lt}= e~r~`:
 .. Let `value` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e~l~` in environment `env`].
 .. Let `literal` be `value`, which must be a `literal`.
 .. `literal` must be the integer `i~l~`.
 .. Let `value'` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e~r~` in environment `env`].
 .. Let `literal'` be `value'`, which must be a `literal`.
 .. `literal'` must be the integer `i~r~`.
 .. Let `b` be `i~l~` is less than or equal to `i~r~`.
 .. Result in the boolean `b`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `e~l~ {amp}{amp} e~r~`:
 .. Let `value` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e~l~` in environment `env`].
 .. Let `literal` be `value`, which must be a `literal`.
 .. `literal` must be the boolean `b~l~`.
 .. Let `value'` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e~r~` in environment `env`].
 .. Let `literal'` be `value'`, which must be a `literal`.
 .. `literal'` must be the boolean `b~r~`.
 .. Let `b` be `b~l~` and `b~r~`.
 .. Result in the boolean `b`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `FUN (t~arg~ x~arg~) -> t~ret~ +++{+++e+++}+++`:
 .. Result in `CLO x~arg~ e env`.
. Otherwise, if `expr` is `e~f~ (e~a~)`:
 .. Let `value` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e~f~` in environment `env`].
 .. `value` must be `CLO x~a~ e~body~ env~clo~`.
 .. Let `v` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e~a~` in environment `env`].
 .. Let `v~ret~` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e~body~` in environment `x~a~ → v {two-colons} env~clo~`].
 .. Result in `v~ret~`.
****

=== Commands

[[Eval_command]]
[.sidebar-title]
****
xref:Eval_command[Eval_command]:

* Executing `command` in environment `env`.
****

****
. If `command` is `SKIP`:
 .. Result in `env`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `t x = e`:
 .. Let `v` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e` in environment `env`].
 .. Result in `x → v {two-colons} env`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `x = e`:
 .. Let `v` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e` in environment `env`].
 .. Result in `x → v {two-colons} env`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `IF e THEN c~1~ ELSE c~2~ END`:
 .. Let `value` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e` in environment `env`].
 .. If `value` is equal to the boolean `true`:
  ... Let `env~1~` be the result of xref:Eval_command[executing `c~1~` in environment `env`].
  ... Result in `env~1~`.
 .. Otherwise, if `value` is equal to the boolean `false`:
  ... Let `env~2~` be the result of xref:Eval_command[executing `c~2~` in environment `env`].
  ... Result in `env~2~`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `WHILE e DO c END`:
 .. Let `value` be the result of xref:Eval_expr[evaluating `e` in environment `env`].
 .. If `value` is equal to the boolean `false`:
  ... Result in `env`.
 .. Otherwise, if `value` is equal to the boolean `true`:
  ... Let `env~1~` be the result of xref:Eval_command[executing `c` in environment `env`].
  ... Let `env~2~` be the result of xref:Eval_command[executing `WHILE e DO c END` in environment `env~1~`].
  ... Result in `env~2~`.
. Otherwise, if `command` is `c~1~ ; c~2~`:
 .. Let `env~1~` be the result of xref:Eval_command[executing `c~1~` in environment `env`].
 .. Let `env~2~` be the result of xref:Eval_command[executing `c~2~` in environment `env~1~`].
 .. Result in `env~2~`.
****
